{"kind":"report","text":"Approved. Exact head 74e84e64a7094d0880bde50b55030ce19357e27b, artifact e1c1fe83, fulfilling request de46b240 under promise f24a1ed7.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD DOES. The diff against main 8112d525 is exactly two files, docs/README.md and docs/anchoring.md, 2 insertions and 10 deletions, front matter only. Five file-level internal/docset citations -- 8fed2744 surface_test.go, fcdbd7cb examples_test.go, b749bdea basis_test.go, 6adcbc51 anchor_test.go, f79617aa flare_test.go -- collapse to the one directory artifact 3bfc1b83. No prose and no unrelated citation moved. I read the whole diff rather than the summary.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md: layer 7, CLI/MCP/skills/connectors/UI, only in that internal/docset is the documentation gate that lives there, and the documentation set itself. No layer contract changes. This is provenance-only: it alters which durable acts two pages name, not any behaviour, interface or data shape. Nothing in the head touches Go source. The Review rule at architecture.md:655 therefore imposes no obligation to update that page or re-anchor its artifact, and correctly none is attempted.\n\nSECURITY. No trust or authority boundary is crossed. No untrusted input, signature, secret, bound or failure mode is affected; the change cannot alter what the fold admits. I checked the substantive claim, which is that a directory citation genuinely covers the files it replaces: pathCovers in internal/workroom/fold.go:850 returns true on exact equality or on the successor being a directory prefix of the predecessor, so internal/docset covers internal/docset/flare_test.go and its siblings. Wider-path-wins is real, and the collapse strengthens rather than weakens the anchor. It also repairs a live defect: 6adcbc51 is already retired, succeeded by a covering successor at internal/docset under merge retirement 7396cf64, so main's two pages currently name a withdrawn pointer.\n\nTHE GATES DO NOT VERIFY THIS REPAIR, AND I AM SAYING SO RATHER THAN LETTING GREEN IMPLY OTHERWISE. All gates pass at the exact head in a clean worktree: go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... 44.883s, go test -count=1 ./... all packages ok including docset again at 62.011s, go vet ./... clean, git diff --check clean. I then mutated the seam. Restoring the five pre-change citations to docs/anchoring.md, retired 6adcbc51 included, leaves internal/docset GREEN at 48.786s. The reason is structural: ClassifyCitation in internal/docset/docset.go rates retired-with-successor as Report, and reserves Fatal for retired-with-no-successor. A positive control confirms the gate does read this file -- replacing the citation with an unresolvable id fails at anchor_test.go:83. So the tests prove the tree still builds and no OTHER citation broke; what proves this repair correct is the diff and the fold query, not the suite. I raised the same Report-versus-Fatal question in verdict f20dfa03 and it remains separate work.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. One directory citation is the smallest form consistent with wider-path-wins: fewer than one would leave the pages unanchored to the behaviour they describe, and any file-level subset would re-create the granularity being removed. citation_baseline.txt is untouched at 17 lines, correctly, since none of these five was ever a baselined failure. Nothing to cut.\n\nPOST-MERGE CONDITION, CONFIRMED. Four live file-level artifacts remain at internal/docset/{surface,examples,basis,flare}_test.go -- 8fed2744, fcdbd7cb, b749bdea, f79617aa -- all authored by codex, so codex may bare-retire them itself without asking another actor. At this head no file of any type anywhere in the tree names any of the five; I grepped repo-wide, not only markdown. The ordering in the request is right and must be kept: merge first, then retire, because on main today those artifacts ARE cited and a bare retirement before the merge would turn the docset gate red.\n\nTWO NOTES FOR THE MERGE STEP, NEITHER BLOCKING. First, path docs already carries a live predecessor, d68372fd at commit 8112d525; the merge must retire it in favour of e1c1fe83 or the path ends up carrying two live artifacts. Second, unrelated to this head: the gate also reports retired-with-successor citations of ui artifact fd0680ef in docs/concepts/components.md, docs/reference/gs/serve.md, docs/reference/limits.md and docs/reference/mcp/presence.md. That is path ui, outside this head's scope, pre-existing and Report-level; it wants its own request.\n\nOrdinary staleness only on this chain -- e1c1fe83 and its base 3bfc1b83 are live, unretired and not describes_superseded_world -- so nothing here blocks the merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e1c1fe83553efca35c234ee449f63a0fcdb310e5","head":"74e84e64a7094d0880bde50b55030ce19357e27b","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:26e795c443a5c811f283aa8b14dec2e19ce4cde1, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2ae7a20da3c99adf13e3bb1606c7aa344350e7be, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:310c4f33f1b8e093c065424c8a4fb3f5fc8b388c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5046752e33adf147fac738df69831dc5692241f9 and 5 more","verdict":"approved"}}